{"text": "Now there's a second problem, and it's you. Your hands are never perfectly still. Your pulse, your breathing, the press of your thumb, all of it makes the phone tremble by tiny fractions of a degree. On a wide shot, you'd never notice. But zoom in, and that tremble is magnified along with everything else. During the exposure, the image smears across the sensor, and a point of light becomes a scribble. Big cameras fix this by moving a group of lens elements. Early phone cameras did the same thing. But glass is heavy, and heavy things are slow to move. So here, the lens stays put, and the lightest part moves instead: the sensor. The silicon chip isn't bolted down. It hangs on fine springs, surrounded by copper coils and small permanent magnets. Run a current through a coil, and it pushes against its magnet, sliding the sensor sideways, or up and down. A gyroscope feels the phone tilt one way. Before the blur can form, the coils shove the sensor so that it follows the image exactly. The body shakes. The picture, as far as the sensor is concerned, never moves. And this isn't one correction. It's a loop: measure, move, check the position, and do it again, thousands of times every second, far faster than any tremor in your hand. It's the same idea as noise-cancelling headphones: sense the disturbance, and produce its exact opposite. The payoff is time. With the image pinned in place, the shutter can stay open several times longer without blur, and a longer exposure means more light. And as we're about to see, for a sensor this small, light is everything.", "alignment": {"characters": ["N", "o", "w", " ", "t", "h", "e", "r", "e", "'", "s", " ", "a", " ", "s", "e", "c", "o", "n", "d", " ", "p", "r", "o", "b", "l", "e", "m", ",", " ", "a", "n", "d", " ", "i", "t", "'", "s", " ", "y", "o", "u", ".", " ", "Y", "o", "u", "r", " ", "h", "a", "n", "d", "s", " ", "a", "r", "e", " ", "n", "e", "v", "e", "r", " ", "p", "e", "r", "f", "e", "c", "t", "l", "y", " ", "s", "t",